course: dictionary
lesson: 6468
title: Nationality
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Your nationality is the country you belong to by law, the one written on your passport. «Her nationality is Irish» tells us which country she is a citizen of. On forms you write your nationality, such as British, American or Australian.
explain: Nationality can also mean a group of people who share the same culture, language or history. «Many nationalities live in the city» means people from lots of different backgrounds live there, not just one kind of person.
explain: Check the context. If a form asks for your nationality, it wants a country. If a text says «people of many nationalities», it is describing a mixed group. Ask yourself: is this about a country, or about a group of people?
fact: A nationality is the country or group of people you belong to.
fact: «Dual nationality» means belonging to two countries by law.
fact: The plural is nationalities, as in «people of all nationalities».
fact: In English, nationality words such as British, Irish and Japanese start with a capital letter.
fact: In French, nationality words are written with a small letter, as in «un garçon français».
exercise: «Her nationality is Irish.» What does nationality mean here? | *The country she belongs to by law | The colour of her hair | Her favourite food
exercise: «Many nationalities live in the city.» What does this mean? | Nobody lives in the city | *People from many different countries and cultures live there | Only one kind of person lives there | The city is very large
exercise: What does «dual nationality» mean? | Having two jobs at the same time | Being born twice | *Belonging to two countries by law | Two kinds of food
test: On a form you write your nationality. Which one is a nationality? | Sunday | Crocodile | Tall | *Australian
test: Which sentence is written correctly in English? | she is japanese. | *She is Japanese. | She is japanese. | she is Japanese.
exam: «People of many nationalities» at a school most likely means | Children who all wear the same uniform | Children who are very tall | *Children from different countries and cultures | Children who sing the same song
